assertion  XSH.2.4.2-realtime-signals.001
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.2
class      required
since      2008
says       A timer created with SIGEV_SIGNAL, SIGRTMIN (SA_SIGINFO set) and
           sigev_value 99 generates SIGRTMIN when it expires, accepted with
           si_code SI_TIMER and si_value 99.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/001-sigev-signal.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.2-realtime-signals.002
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.2
class      required
since      2008
says       A timer created with SIGEV_THREAD (attributes NULL) calls
           sigev_notify_function with sigev_value (a pointer) when it
           expires, in a thread other than the main thread.
nomutant   the notification thread is made by the system
test       t/002-sigev-thread.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.2-realtime-signals.003
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.2
class      required
since      2008
says       A timer created with SIGEV_NONE generates no signal when it
           expires (none of SIGRTMIN, SIGALRM pending 300 ms after), and
           timer_gettime() then shows it disarmed.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/003-sigev-none.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.2-realtime-signals.004
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.2
class      required
since      2008
says       With SIGRTMIN blocked (SA_SIGINFO set) and values 1 and 2 queued by
           sigqueue(), a kill() of SIGRTMIN does not disturb them: the
           first two accepted are SI_QUEUE with values 1 and 2.
uses       XSH.sigtimedwait.001
note       Linux queues kill()-generated realtime signals too, after the
           earlier ones, which is allowed.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/004-kill-keeps-queue.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.2-realtime-signals.005
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.2
class      required
since      2008
says       With SIGRTMIN caught with SA_SIGINFO and two values queued while
           blocked, on unblocking the first handler call still sees
           SIGRTMIN pending (one more queued), and after the second call it
           is not pending.
note       sigpending() is async-signal-safe; the handler blocks
           SIGRTMIN while it runs, so the second occurrence waits
           pending.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/005-pending-while-queued.c
